The parent process is represented in kernel memory by a  C struct called a task_struct
signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_IGN); // signal() function  executes much earlier. When it ran, it made a system call that modified a lookup array inside its (c_->sighand->action[].)
-> The moment the child process finishes or crashes, the kernel executes do_exit() on behalf of the child.
-> Inside do_exit(), the kernel transitions to a function called exit_notify(). The kernel follows a memory pointer from the dying child to find its parent's data structure:
struct task_struct *parent = current->real_parent; in this struct kernel looks at array index reserved for SIGCHLD:-
struct k_sigaction *action = &parent->sighand->action[SIGCHLD - 1];  //  value at this index is set to SIG_INT by signal,
-> now kernel runs:-
if (action->sa.sa_handler == SIG_IGN) {
    // 1. Mark the child as EXIT_DEAD instead of EXIT_ZOMBIE
    current->exit_state = EXIT_DEAD;
    
    // 2. Immediately call release_task() to erase the child from the process tree
    release_task(current); 
    
    // 3. Skip sending any signal notifications to the parent.
    return; 
} 
else { // if array was unmodified:-
    // 1. Mark the child as a Zombie
    current->exit_state = EXIT_ZOMBIE;
    
    // 2. Deliver the SIGCHLD signal to the parent's mailbox
    do_notify_parent(current, current->exit_signal);
    
    // 3. Wake up the parent if it is currently sleeping/waiting for a child
    if (parent->state == TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE) {
        wake_up_process(parent);
    }
->If the parent process was suspended or waiting inside a blocking wait() or waitpid() loop, the kernel flips the parent's state back to "Running" so
it can immediately wake up and handle the incoming data.

